<!--
    Control styles.

    The first pass looked like a Win32 app, and the reasons were specific: every button
    carried a visible border, selection used the system blue highlight, hard 1px dividers
    separated everything, and the rail used colour emoji. Modern Windows chrome is the
    opposite of all four - borderless surfaces separated by tone rather than lines,
    selection expressed as a tinted fill plus an accent bar, and a single monochrome icon
    font that inherits the foreground colour.

    Four controls were left out of that argument and kept every problem it describes, which is
    worth stating because the omission was invisible: nothing fails when a control is simply
    never styled.

    - CheckBox and RadioButton were WPF's own Aero glyphs, on a dark surface, in the settings
      popup and beside "Show full file". Both are implicit styles now - keyed by type, named by
      nothing, which is the only way to reach controls whose call sites do not mention a style.
    - TreeViewItem, twice. ListBoxItem was retemplated here BECAUSE the system highlight is the
      loudest tell, and the project tree is not a ListBox - so it kept highlight-blue selection
      and the Aero expander triangle, and the history tree inherited the same the day it was
      added. TreeRowItem is RowItem's equivalent for both.
    - ToolTip, which is a Popup and therefore reachable from no window's visual tree at all.

    The pattern in all four: an implicit style, or a keyed one a container asks for, and a test
    that applies and MEASURES it - a key can hold a template that resolves fine and throws the
    first time something renders it.

    WHERE THE STYLES THEMSELVES LIVE.

    This file is the running order and nothing else; it held all of it until the single
    dictionary reached 1,228 lines and every style edit produced a diff touching all of them.

      Primitives   the two converters, the icon font and its glyph strings, IconText, the
                   Motion storyboards, and the two focus rings. Everything below reaches
                   into this and nothing here reaches back out.
      Buttons      buttons, and the navigation rail built from them.
      Inputs       combo boxes, text boxes, check boxes and radio buttons.
      Rows         rows in a list, and rows in a tree.
      ScrollBars   scrollbars on both axes, and the splitter between panes.
      Chrome       section labels, the monospace run, tooltips and chips.
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        EACH DICTIONARY BELOW MERGES Primitives.xaml FOR ITSELF, AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANT.

        A StaticResource in a merged dictionary resolves against that dictionary and its own
        merges - never against a sibling the parent merged alongside it. So merging Primitives
        first here does NOT put FocusRing or Motion.MarkIn within reach of Buttons.xaml; only
        Buttons.xaml merging it does. This is the trap written up in the paragraph above, and
        it costs a XamlParseException at render time from a file that compiled clean.

        Primitives is merged here as well, on top of that, so views outside this folder -
        MainWindow and the dialogs - find the icon glyphs and the two converters through
        Application.Resources.

        StyleSheetResolutionTests renders every keyed style in the merged result, which is
        what turns a missing merge back into a build-time answer instead of a runtime one.
    -->
    <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
        <ResourceDictionary Source="Primitives.xaml" />
        <ResourceDictionary Source="Buttons.xaml" />
        <ResourceDictionary Source="Inputs.xaml" />
        <ResourceDictionary Source="Rows.xaml" />
        <ResourceDictionary Source="ScrollBars.xaml" />
        <ResourceDictionary Source="Chrome.xaml" />
    </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>

</ResourceDictionary>
